  "summary": "The code suffered from a critical N+1 query performance issue and lacked proper documentation and type safety. I have refactored the data retrieval to use a single JOIN query, added type hints, and included a docstring. Overall code health is significantly improved by reducing database round-trips.",
  "fixed_code": "import sqlite3\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Any\n\ndef load_dashboards(conn: sqlite3.Connection, user_ids: List[int]) -> List[Dict[str, Any]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Fetches user and order data for a list of user IDs in a single batch operation.\n\n    Args:\n        conn: SQLite connection object.\n        user_ids: List of user IDs to fetch.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of dictionaries containing user and order information.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not user_ids or len(user_ids) > 1000:\n        return []\n\n    placeholders = ','.join(['?'] * len(user_ids))\n    query = f\"\"\"\n        SELECT u.id, u.name, o.id, o.total \n        FROM users u \n        LEFT JOIN orders o ON u.id = o.user_id \n        WHERE u.id IN ({placeholders})\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    rows = conn.execute(query, user_ids).fetchall()\n    \n    dashboards = {}\n    for u_id, u_name, o_id, o_total in rows:\n        if u_id not in dashboards:\n            dashboards[u_id] = {\"user\": (u_id, u_name), \"orders\": []}\n        if o_id is not None:\n            dashboards[u_id][\"orders\"].append((o_id, o_total))\n            \n    return list(dashboards.values())"
